#: {{alti|en|lac<qq:dated>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Thomas Coryat|chapter=[Coryats Letters.] From the Court of the Great Mogul, Resident at the Towne of Asinere in the Eastern India, on Michaelmas Day. Anno 1615.|title=[[w:Coryat's Crudities|Coryat’s Crudities; {{...|Reprinted from the Edition of 1611.}} To which are Now Added, His Letters from India, &c.{{nb...|and Extracts Relating to Him, from Various Authors: Being a More Particular Account of His Travels (Mostly on Foot) in Different Parts of the Globe, than any hitherto Published. Together with His Orations, Character, Death, &c. With Copper-plates. In Three Volumes.}}]]|volume=III|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} W. Cater,{{nb...|No. 274, Holborn}}; J. Wilkie,{{nb...|No. 71, St. Paul’s Church-yard}}; and E. Easton,{{nb...|at Salisbury.}}|year='''1615''' September|year_published=1776|pages=158–159|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=UgoqiWDoyvQC&pg=PA159|oclc=1264843908|passage=Since my arriual heere, there vvas ſent vnto this King one of the richeſt preſents that I haue heard to be ſent to any Prince in all my life time: {{...}} the vvhole Preſent vvas vvorth ten of their '''Leakes''', as they call them; a '''Leak''' being ten thouſand pound ſterling: the vvhole, a hundred thouſand pounds ſterling.}}
#* {{RQ:Purchas Pilgrimes|part=1|chapter=Captaine [[w:William Hawkins (fl. c. 1600)|William Hawkins]], His Relations of the Occurrents which Happened in the Time of His Residence in India, in the County of the Great Mogoll, and of His Departure from thence; Written to the [[w:East India Company|[East India] Company]]|page=217|section=IIII|passage=I''Nprimis'', of ''Rupias Ecbery'', thirteene ''[[crore|Crou]]'' (euery ''Crou'' is an hundred '''''Leckes''''', and euery '''''Leck''''' an hundred thouſand ''Rupias'') or one thouſand three hundred '''''Leckes'''''.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Virginians|volume=I|chapter=Fortunatus Nimum|page=331|passage=Who has not admired that noble speech of my Lord [[w:Robert Clive|{{quote-gloss|Robert}} Clive]], when reproached on his return from India with making rather too free with jaghires, '''lakhs''', gold mohurs, diamonds, pearls, and what not: "Upon my life," said the hero of Plassy, "when I think of my opportunities, I am surprised I took so little!"}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} ''Often in the {{glossary|plural}}'': an [[indefinitely]] [[large#Adjective|large]] [[number#Noun|number]]; a [[zillion]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:zillion}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Robert Montgomery Martin|R[obert] Montgomery Martin]]|quotee=w:Yashwantrao Holkar|chapter=Section II. European Intercourse—Rise and Growth of British Power.|title=The Indian Empire:{{nb...|History, Topography, Geology, Climate, Population, Chief Cities and Provinces; Tributary and Protected States; Military Power and Resources; Religion, Education, Crime; Land Tenures; Staple Products; Government, Finance, and Commerce. With a Full Account of the Mutiny of the Bengal Army; of the Insurrection in Western India; and an Exposition of the Alleged Causes.}}|location=London&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=The London Printing and Publishing Company|year=1804|year_published=[1858]|volume=!I (History, Topography, Population, Government, Finance, Commerce, and Staple Products)|page=399|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.36987/page/n426/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=552048115|passage={{...}} Lake {{quote-gloss|''i.e.'', {{w|Gerard Lake, 1st Viscount Lake}}}} should not have leisure to breathe for a moment, and calamities would fall on '''lacs''' of human beings in continued war by the attacks of his army, which would overwhelm like the waves of the sea.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Contemporary Portraits. New Series.—No. 8. {{w|Charles Darwin}}, F.R.S.|journal=[[w:Dublin University Magazine|The University Magazine: A Literary and Philosophic Review]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hurst and Blackett|Hurst & Blackett]],{{nb...}}|month=August|year=1878|volume=II|page=154|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=kNEvAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA154|oclc=828212439|passage=The Laccadives and Maldives, for instance, meaning literally the "'''lac''' of islands" and the "thousand islands," are a series of such atolls; {{...}}}}

